{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>If he says thus, speckled shall be your wages, etc.</b> It should have said ״אִם כֹּה אָמַר,״ for ״יֹאמַר״ connotes the future. And it appears that this refers to what he said, ״וְלֹא נְתָנוֹ אֱלֹהִים לְהָרַע עִמָּדִי״ (Genesis 31:7), for it had already appeared to him in a dream: ״רָאִיתִי אֵת כָּל אֲשֶׁר לָבָן עֹשֶׂה לָּךָ״ (Genesis 31:12). Therefore he said ״אִם כֹּה יֹאמַר״ — for the future, for it is obvious that at the time of setting the rods they would give birth to ring-streaked and speckled; but even for the future, when I do not set the rods, nevertheless everything will be according to his stipulation: if he says ״speckled shall be your wages,״ they will all give birth speckled — for thus I was promised in a dream from the mouth of the Gevurah, because HaShem saw my affliction, all that Laban is doing to me. For when I said ״הָסֵר מִשָּׁם כָּל שֶׂה נָקֹד וְטָלוּא״ (Genesis 30:32), my intention was that there should be two flocks adjacent to one another, and if the females go to the flock of speckled and ring-streaked, my mazal caused it, and if they conceive by their own flock, your mazal caused it. And presumably Laban would agree to this, for it is more likely to him that they would conceive by their own flock than that they would go to another flock. But Laban distanced a three-day journey, so as to prevent the females from going to that flock, in order that Jacob should go away empty-handed. Therefore it was necessary to perform the business with the rods, and the Holy One, blessed be He, agreed through his hand to say that even without setting the rods it would be so for the future." "And in the book Toldot Yitzhak he explained that this is why ״אִם כֹּה יֹאמַר״ is said — because Laban saw that whatever Jacob desired, HaShem did and agreed through his hand. Therefore he thought to devise a stratagem against the will of the Blessed El, namely to stipulate speckled first, and when they conceived speckled, then he would turn around and stipulate ring-streaked — for they had already conceived speckled. But the Holy One, blessed be He, knew what was in Laban's heart, that he would say this after they conceived, and the Holy One, blessed be He, arranged that they conceived from that appearance which Laban would say at the time of conception, ״in this appearance I desire״ — therefore ״אִם כֹּה יֹאמַר״ is said, for the future."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:31:21.441486200Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}